From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:21:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] kk: MESA_KK_FAULT_SPLIT commits each encoder alone,
 MESA_KK_FAULT_SKIP leaves out passes by kind and copies, and fault checks its
 timestamps

The third phone repro of The Witcher 3 1.32's GPU hang (PLA-880) failed
with the same command buffer as the first two: three G-buffer passes, the
5-layer passes, the x5-instance pass and a copy. Every encoder said
"not-started", and the hang came 27 ms after the GPU began the command
buffer. Two things kept that run from naming the encoder:
- Nothing ever showed that the encoder timestamps work: they are read only
  after a failure, and a commit tick on another clock than the counter
  heap's would turn every encoder into "not-started".
- The suspect pipelines' MSL hashes were new. That is not a per-run seed:
  0157 added a field to the root table's draw part, which moved push
  constants and every later field from byte 672 to 680, so every shader
  reading them has new MSL. A hash prefix holds for one build only.

With MESA_KK_DEBUG=fault:
- MESA_KK_FAULT_SPLIT=N (the game's Graphics options) ends a command
  buffer's Metal command buffer after every N encoders and begins another on
  the same allocator; the queue commits each one alone, in order, with a
  feedback of its own. A failure then says "cb #C part p of P (enc i)
  failed" and lists only that part's encoders, so the failing encoder is
  named without timestamps or hashes. MESA_KK_FAULT_SPLIT=draw also ends the
  render encoder after every draw (as a compute encoder inside a pass does),
  so each draw is a commit of its own. A command buffer keeps the extra
  Metal command buffers it took for the next recording and gives them back
  to its pool when it is freed.
- MESA_KK_FAULT_SKIP also takes layered (draws in passes of more than one
  layer), instanced (direct draws of more than one instance), copy (buffer
  and image copies) and WxH (draws in passes of that render area): a
  suspect is left out by its shape instead of its hash. The [kk-fault] on:
  line now says "leaving out:", and names the split.
- The first three completed command buffers with encoder timestamps log
  "timestamp check: cb #C: S of E encoder(s) wrote a start and D an end;
  enc i start tick T, commit tick K, F Hz: the starts follow|precede the
  commit". It shows in any run whether the GPU writes the timestamps, and
  whether the commit tick a failure's gpu= times and not-started count from
  is on the same clock.

Without MESA_KK_DEBUG=fault none of this runs, and nothing changes.

Linear: PLA-880
Class: diagnostics
Evidence: build/mesa-host-test/kk-fault-test.c (mock Metal: each encoder, or each two, committed as a Metal command buffer of its own and named by a failure; a draw a render encoder; the skips by kind, area and copy; the timestamp check on both clocks); PLA-880 (phone3 repro: every encoder not-started, new hashes)
Offered-upstream: no
